CERTORA VERIFICATION MUNGE — drop the free-memory-pointer reset in the event ops
=================================================================================
splitOnEvent / mergeOnEvent / convertOnEvent each capture the free-memory pointer
before their fan-out loop and `mstore(0x40, memoryCheckpoint)` it back after every
iteration. That is a pure GAS optimization: it lets each iteration reuse the memory
the previous one allocated (the transient child-legs array), keeping memory flat
instead of growing linearly (quadratic memory-expansion gas) over the event's
conditions.

The plain (non-"memory-safe") assembly rewind collapses the Prover's memory model
into a spurious always-revert, so all three methods verify VACUOUSLY (rule_sanity:
SANITY_FAIL). Confirmed by a controlled experiment (a reset-free harness clone is
reachable while the real method is not — job 96f08b37).

Removing the reset is behavior-identical for the state-based solvency/canonicity
properties:
  - the child-legs array is copied into STORAGE (_storeLegsFromMemory) and its id is
    consumed into the mint BEFORE the reset — nothing from iteration i is read in i+1;
  - parentLegs / baseLegs / childPositionIds (needed across iterations) are allocated
    BEFORE the loop, below the checkpoint, so the rewind never touches them;
  - each iteration allocates fresh, zero-initialized arrays, so reuse cannot alias a
    stale read.
The only observable difference is memory-expansion GAS, which the Prover does not
model. Storage writes, mints, burns and revert conditions are unchanged. (Validated
by mutation testing on the event ops.)

Required by: CombinatorialCanonicalEvents / CombinatorialWellFormedEvents confs.
Apply:  git apply certora/patches/CombinatorialModule_eventMemoryReset.patch
Revert: git apply -R certora/patches/CombinatorialModule_eventMemoryReset.patch
Never commit the applied state to src/.

